==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|chastize}} (archaic in British English and rare in American English)

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|chastisen}}, from {{der|en|fro|chastier}}, from {{der|en|la|castīgō}}. See also the doublets {{doublet|en|chasten|castigate|notext=1}} and cf. also {{l|en|chaste}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/tʃæˈstaɪz/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-chastise.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃæstaɪz/|/t͡ʃæˈstaɪz/}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪz|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[punish]] (someone), especially by [[corporal punishment]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1809|author=Flavius Josephus|title=The first eleven books of the Antiquities of the Jews, with a table of the Jewish coins, weights and measures|page=402
|passage=and if his father had '''chastised''' them with whips, they must expect that he would do it with scorpions.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=Nyx Smith|title=Shadowrun Legends: Striper Assassin|publisher=Catalyst Game Labs
|passage=“There is a man who must be '''chastised''',” she says softly, lightly. “'''Chastised''' in a physical way. It should not be difficult. Not for you.”}}
# To [[castigate]]; to [[severely]] [[scold]] or [[censure]] (someone).

====Synonyms====
* See also [[Thesaurus:criticize]]

